theory.intro:
A **rectilinear figure** is made only of straight sides meeting at right angles. Its opposite sides balance: what runs one way must total the same as what runs back the other way. That balance finds an unlabelled side.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (6,0) node[midway, below] {$a$};
\draw (6,0) -- (6,2) node[midway, right] {$q$};
\draw (6,2) -- (3,2) node[midway, above] {$c$};
\draw (3,2) -- (3,5) node[midway, right] {$r$};
\draw (3,5) -- (0,5) node[midway, above] {$b$};
\draw (0,5) -- (0,0) node[midway, left] {$p$};
\node at (3,-1.1) {$a = b + c$};
\node at (3,-1.9) {$p = q + r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A side that steps **inwards** faces the opposite way, so it counts on the other side of the balance.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (6,0);
\draw (6,0) -- (6,3) node[midway, right] {$q$};
\draw (6,3) -- (4,3);
\draw (4,3) -- (4,1) node[midway, left] {$s$};
\draw (4,1) -- (2,1);
\draw (2,1) -- (2,5) node[midway, right] {$t$};
\draw (2,5) -- (0,5);
\draw (0,5) -- (0,0) node[midway, left] {$p$};
\node at (3,-1.2) {$q + t = p + s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

